      "message": "**Follow the provided skill first** — Use the skill named `infographic-creator` as your primary workflow. Each skill has its own generation approach that produces higher-quality results. Leverage the skill's design system, templates, and rendering pipeline to maximize output quality.\n\n# instruction.md\n\n## Poster Brief: PYPL Programming Language Popularity Index — April 2026\n\nYou're creating a portrait data-report poster that gives developers and tech decision-makers a clear, at-a-glance understanding of which programming languages are rising and falling in 2026. The core narrative is simple: **Python dominates, C/C++ is surging, and Java is in steep decline.** The poster should feel like a polished, modern infographic — the kind a developer would screenshot and share on social media.\n\n### Key Priorities\n\n- **The Top 10 rankings table is the centerpiece.** It should be the largest, most prominent element — readers need to scan the hierarchy instantly. Use clear rank numbers, share percentages, and directional trend indicators (arrows or color-coded change values).\n- **The three headline metrics — Python (36.21%), C/C++ (13.21%), Java (10.01%) — should be visually dominant** near the top, styled as bold stat callouts so the power dynamics are obvious before anyone reads the table.\n- **Gainers vs. losers contrast matters.** Use green/red or equivalent accent colors to make positive and negative trends pop at a glance throughout the poster.\n\n### Visual & Style Guidance\n\nAim for a **dark-mode, developer-aesthetic** feel — think deep navy or charcoal backgrounds with vibrant accent colors (electric blue, bright green for gains, warm red/orange for losses). Typography should be clean and monospaced or semi-monospaced for data elements, paired with a modern sans-serif for headings. Keep whitespace generous; avoid clutter. The tone is authoritative and data-forward — no playful illustrations, just sharp data visualization and clean hierarchy.\n\nThe methodology note and emerging languages section should be present but visually secondary — smaller type, lower placement.\n\n---\n\n## Data Sources\n\n1. Read `/app/benchmark/source_brief.md` for all content materials (text, data, key messages).\n2. Read `/app/benchmark/task_input.json` for poster requirements, sections, metrics, and style definitions.\n\n## Output Requirements\n\n1. Output the final poster to `/app/output/final_poster.png`.\n2. The poster must be a single self-contained image file.\n\n## Automated Execution Rules\n\nThere is no human operator online for this session. Follow these rules:\n\n1. **Skip all confirmation/approval/user-input steps** — do not pause, ask questions, or wait for a reply. When encountering BLOCKING or wait-for-user instructions, make a professional judgment and continue.\n2. **Design decisions** — if `task_input.json` specifies `poster.tone`, `poster.audience`, etc., use them to choose templates, palettes, and styles. Otherwise, make professional judgments.\n3. **Priority** — these rules override any interactive-wait instructions in SKILL.md. The skill's workflow, quality standards, and technical specs remain fully effective — only skip interactive waits."
